Here the text windows and the pattern get a rolling hash. Updating the next window only removes one character and adds one character, so checking every window is quick.

A hash match is followed by a direct character comparison. That final check keeps the result correct even if two different strings happen to have the same hash.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        vector <int> search(string pat, string txt){
            vector<int> result;
            int m = pat.size();
            int n = txt.size();

            if (m == 0 || m > n) {
                return result;
            }

            const long long mod = 1000000007LL;
            const long long base = 911382323LL;
            long long patternHash = 0;
            long long windowHash = 0;
            long long highestPower = 1;

            for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
                patternHash = (patternHash * base + pat[i]) % mod;
                windowHash = (windowHash * base + txt[i]) % mod;
                if (i + 1 < m) {
                    highestPower = (highestPower * base) % mod;
                }
            }

            for (int start = 0; start <= n - m; ++start) {
                if (patternHash == windowHash) {
                    bool same = true;
                    for (int j = 0; j < m; ++j) {
                        if (pat[j] != txt[start + j]) {
                            same = false;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    if (same) {
                        result.push_back(start);
                    }
                }

                if (start + m < n) {
                    long long removed = (txt[start] * highestPower) % mod;
                    windowHash = (windowHash - removed + mod) % mod;
                    windowHash = (windowHash * base + txt[start + m]) % mod;
                }
            }

            return result;
        }
};
```

The rolling part is `O(n)`, but the verification step can make the worst case `O(nm)`. Space use is `O(1)` besides the returned indexes. Very repetitive strings are the main case where the direct checks add extra work.

This keeps two pointers, one for the text and one for the pattern. When characters differ, the LPS table skips pattern characters that have already matched.

A useful detail is resetting `patternIndex` after a match instead of setting it to zero. That is what allows cases like `"aaa"` inside `"aaaaa"` to return indexes `0`, `1`, and `2`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> search(String pat, String txt) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int patternLength = pat.length();
        int textLength = txt.length();

        if (patternLength == 0 || patternLength > textLength) {
            return result;
        }

        int[] lps = new int[patternLength];
        for (int i = 1, prefixLength = 0; i < patternLength; ) {
            if (pat.charAt(i) == pat.charAt(prefixLength)) {
                lps[i] = ++prefixLength;
                ++i;
            } else if (prefixLength > 0) {
                prefixLength = lps[prefixLength - 1];
            } else {
                lps[i] = 0;
                ++i;
            }
        }

        int textIndex = 0;
        int patternIndex = 0;
        while (textIndex < textLength) {
            if (txt.charAt(textIndex) == pat.charAt(patternIndex)) {
                ++textIndex;
                ++patternIndex;

                if (patternIndex == patternLength) {
                    result.add(textIndex - patternLength);
                    patternIndex = lps[patternIndex - 1];
                }
            } else if (patternIndex > 0) {
                patternIndex = lps[patternIndex - 1];
            } else {
                ++textIndex;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The scan takes `O(n + m)` time, while the LPS array uses `O(m)` space. An empty pattern returns an empty list here.

The pattern is searched without moving the text index backward. The LPS array tells us how much of the pattern is still useful after a mismatch.

For example, with `pat = "aba"` and `txt = "ababa"`, the matches start at indexes `0` and `2`. After finding the first match, the pattern length falls back through the LPS array, so overlapping matches are also found.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        vector <int> search(string pat, string txt){
            vector<int> answer;
            int m = pat.size();
            int n = txt.size();

            if (m == 0 || m > n) {
                return answer;
            }

            vector<int> lps(m, 0);
            for (int i = 1, length = 0; i < m; ) {
                if (pat[i] == pat[length]) {
                    lps[i++] = ++length;
                } else if (length > 0) {
                    length = lps[length - 1];
                } else {
                    lps[i++] = 0;
                }
            }

            for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < n; ) {
                if (txt[i] == pat[j]) {
                    ++i;
                    ++j;

                    if (j == m) {
                        answer.push_back(i - m);
                        j = lps[j - 1];
                    }
                } else if (j > 0) {
                    j = lps[j - 1];
                } else {
                    ++i;
                }
            }

            return answer;
        }
};
```

Time complexity is `O(n + m)`, and the extra space is `O(m)`. The input strings are not changed.

This is the straightforward version. Each possible starting position is checked character by character, and matching indexes are added to the answer. It is slower than prefix-based matching, but the code is easy to test and it naturally includes overlapping matches.

For `pat = "ana"` and `txt = "banana"`, the checks succeed at positions `1` and `3`. The next check starts one position later, so no possible match is skipped.

```python
class Solution:
    def search(self, pat, txt):
        matches = []
        pattern_length = len(pat)
        text_length = len(txt)

        if pattern_length == 0 or pattern_length > text_length:
            return matches

        for start in range(text_length - pattern_length + 1):
            matched = True
            for offset in range(pattern_length):
                if txt[start + offset] != pat[offset]:
                    matched = False
                    break
            if matched:
                matches.append(start)

        return matches
```

The worst-case time is `O((n - m + 1) * m)`, usually written as `O(nm)`. It uses `O(1)` extra space apart from the result list. A pattern longer than the text simply produces no indexes.
